From 3d0d636940eab86106693abc782ead5e8c578425 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:03:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Extend INDEXF.SYS and directories above LBN 4,194,304 (#14)

INDEXF.SYS was only ever extended with Format 1 retrieval pointers,
which can't address an LBN past 4,194,302. On a large VMS-initialized
volume (INITIALIZE's default /INDEX=MIDDLE puts INDEXF.SYS around LBN
33 million on 32 GiB) creating a file failed once the /HEADERS
INITIALIZE preallocated were used up and the free space found lay
above that limit.

The new ods2_indexf_map_extent() maps each new piece of INDEXF.SYS
with the smallest pointer that describes it - Format 1 below the
limit, Format 2 above - checking the map area against the pointer's
real size, and leaving the header unchanged when it doesn't fit.
extend_indexf() uses it and still gives the blocks back on failure.

store_directory() had the same Format 1-only limit when a directory
moves to a larger area; it now uses the smallest pointer too. The
256-block directory cap is unchanged.

ods2_indexf_large_selftest checks Format 1 below the limit, Format 2
above it (decoding back to the same extent, HIBLK and HIGHWATER
raised), refusal with the map area full, and end to end: an INDEXF.SYS
extent at LBN 5,000,000 on a sparse copy of the synthetic disk, with
new headers created there, read back at the expected LBN, valid, and
INDEXF.SYS's header and backup identical.

Not yet checked on VMS: a volume INITIALIZEd with default /INDEX and a
small /HEADERS, filled past them, then ANALYZE/DISK.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
